# The gate's own footprint. This file is the recipe in PLUNK-IN.md step 3, verbatim,
# so this repo is itself an instance of the kit it ships. It is load-bearing, not
# cosmetic: the C++ gate's `tree` stage fails when the gate's own output shows up as an
# untracked file (`git ls-files --others --exclude-standard`), and the stage creates
# .ci-logs and the build dirs before it looks.

# the gate's own footprint
#
# NO TRAILING SLASHES, deliberately. `tree` runs FIRST, so on a fresh clone it asks whether
# these paths are ignored before any stage has created them — and `git check-ignore` cannot
# match a directory-only pattern (`.ci-logs/`, `build/`) against a path that does not exist.
# Measured on git 2.47.3, same path, directory absent: `build/` NOT IGNORED, `build` ignored.
# With the slash, the first run fails the tree stage for a reason its own message cannot
# explain ("the next run would fail on its own log files" — with the rule sitting right here
# in this file) and the second run passes. The fix is guarded by
# probes/gitignore-footprint.sh.
.ci-logs
build
build-*
.ci.env

# python
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# deno
# (nothing: deno.lock belongs in git)

# defensive, beyond the shipped recipe
_deps/
*.log

# NOT ignored, deliberately: .ci/ holds the clang-tidy baseline
# (.ci/tidy-baseline.txt), which is tracked — it is this repo's accepted-findings list
# and the tidy stage compares against it. Ignoring the directory would silently exempt
# that file from the tree stage, which is the failure the stage exists to catch.
# See templates/cpp/.ci.env.example and DESIGN-NOTES.md D11.
